Based on my search, here's the configuration information for **next-intl** with `i18n.ts`, `routing.ts`, and `middleware.ts`:

## File Structure

The recommended file structure is:
```
your-app/
├─ next.config.ts
├─ messages/
│ ├─ en.json
│ └─ de.json
├─ src/
│ ├─ i18n/
│ │ ├─ request.ts
│ │ └─ routing.ts
│ ├─ middleware.ts
│ ├─ app/
│ │ └─ [locale]/
│ │ ├─ layout.tsx
│ │ └─ page.tsx
```

## routing.ts

Use `routing.ts` as a central place to define your routing configuration:
```typescript
import {defineRouting} from 'next-intl/routing';

export const routing = defineRouting({
  // A list of all locales that are supported
  locales: ['en', 'de'],
  // Used when no locale matches
  defaultLocale: 'en'
});
```(no content)You can also create navigation wrappers:
```typescript
import { createNavigation } from 'next-intl/navigation';

export const { Link, redirect, usePathname, useRouter } = createNavigation(routing);
```

## middleware.ts (or proxy.ts)

`proxy.ts` was called `middleware.ts` up until Next.js 16.(no content)Once you have your routing configuration in place, you can use it to set up the middleware:
```typescript
import createMiddleware from 'next-intl/middleware';
import {routing} from './i18n/routing';

export default createMiddleware(routing);

export const config = {
  // Match all pathnames except for
  // - … if they start with `/api`, `/trpc`, `/_next` or `/_vercel`
  // - … the ones containing a dot (e.g. `favicon.ico`)
  matcher: '/((?!api|trpc|_next|_vercel|.*\\..*).*)' 
};
```(no content)The middleware receives a routing configuration and takes care of locale negotiation, applying relevant redirects & rewrites, and providing alternate links for search engines.

## i18n/request.ts

`i18n/request.ts` can be used to provide configuration for server-only code, i.e. Server Components, Server Actions & friends. The configuration is provided via the `getRequestConfig` function and provides a `requestLocale` parameter in case you're using locale-based routing.

```typescript
import {getRequestConfig} from 'next-intl/server';
import {hasLocale} from 'next-intl';
import {routing} from './routing';

export default getRequestConfig(async ({requestLocale}) => {
  const requested = await requestLocale;
  const locale = hasLocale(routing.locales, requested)
    ? requested
    : routing.defaultLocale;

  return {
    locale,
    messages: (await import(`../../messages/${locale}.json`)).default
  };
});
```

## next.config.ts

For internationalization to work in your app, add the next-intl plugin to Next.js:
```typescript
import createNextIntlPlugin from 'next-intl/plugin';
const withNextIntl = createNextIntlPlugin();

const nextConfig = {};
export default withNextIntl(nextConfig);
```


